      "number": 658,
      "title": "release: v0.9.10",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ngit fetch --tags\ngit checkout v0.9.10\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nBumps the workspace to `v0.9.10` and rewrites `RELEASE_NOTES.md` to cover the release. Version strings in `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`, `pyproject.toml`, and `uv.lock` are updated in lockstep.\n\nThe notes span the wave since `v0.9.9`: chord-waves absorbing the old chord model, PM integration across Asana/Linear/Notion, the attention queue and wave workspaces in Concerto, a unified flow engine behind `lf` and `lfd`, OAuth-only PM auth, wave crons and restructured items, the `demo` / `code-review` split, ecosystem imports (gstack, npx), and the decisions-ledger-driven release flow.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Workspace version bumped to `0.9.10` across Rust, Python, and lockfiles\n- `RELEASE_NOTES.md` rewritten for `v0.9.10` with sectioned highlights (waves absorb chords, PM integration, attention queue, wave workspaces, shared execution engine, auth, wave config, review workflow, ecosystem, release/CI, QoL)\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 84,
      "deletions": 16,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 661,
      "title": "release: defensive tag fix + backfill v0.9.10 archive",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ngit checkout jack-heart.release-tag-fix.20260424_1024\ncargo test -p loopflow ops::release::tests::ensure_commit_local\n```\n\n## Intent\n\nTwo things that bit during the v0.9.10 cut:\n\n1. **Tag step failed with \\\"trying to write ref ... with nonexistent object\\\".**\n   \\`tag_and_push_ref\\` resolved the merge-queue commit via \\`git rev-parse <40-char-hex>\\`, which echoes the SHA without verifying the object is actually local. \\`git tag\\` then failed because the local repo hadn't fetched the post-merge state.\n\n2. **release/v0.9.10/ wasn't created.** The cut ran with the installed \\`lf\\` v0.9.9, which predates \\`promote_unreleased_dir\\` / \\`archive_release_notes\\` (added in #636). So PR #658 only contained manifest bumps + RELEASE_NOTES.md, leaving \\`release/unreleased/\\` untouched and no archive.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- **Defensive fix in \\`tag_and_push_ref\\`, not in \\`release_run\\`.** Adds \\`ensure_commit_local\\` (cat-file -e + fetch-on-miss) so any caller of the helper benefits, not just the one-shot release flow. Tests use a second clone to push behind the local repo's back, mirroring the merge-queue race.\n- **Backfill rather than re-cut.** The tag and code on origin are correct; only the on-disk archive is missing. Doing the rename + NOTES copy by hand matches what the current code would have produced.\n\n## Not included\n\n- The GitHub Release workflow for v0.9.10 failed at \\`build-dmg\\` (run 24901972034). No GitHub Release, crates.io publish, or PyPI publish landed. Separate from this PR — flagging here so it isn't lost.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 190,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 662,
      "title": "gstack: bundle as namespaced builtin, simplify step discovery",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf gstack/office-hours            # namespaced builtin\nlf office-hours                   # bare name works when unambiguous\nlf npx/vercel-labs/deep-research  # live-fetch any Claude Skill\nlf refresh-gstack                 # maintainer: resync bundled gstack\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMoves the `gstack` workstyle from `.lf/steps/gstack/` and `.lf/flows/gstack/` into the binary as a first-class namespaced builtin under `rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/gstack/`. Core categories (`build`, `govern`, `ops`) still share one flat namespace; other categories like `gstack` are registered as `<cat>/<name>` and are reachable by bare name when exactly one namespace owns the stem. Namespaced steps and flows now use `/` instead of `:`.\n\nAs part of the cleanup, the bespoke `ops/gstack.rs` loader (454 lines) and the `skill_sources` / `~/.superpowers` auto-detection paths are gone. Step discovery shrinks substantially — `lf/discovery.rs` drops from ~495 lines of source hierarchy to a much thinner resolver that walks repo → user-global → core builtins → namespaced builtins → external skill namespaces (`npx/`, legacy `rams/rams`). A new `refresh-gstack` step documents how maintainers resync the bundled catalog from `garrytan/gstack`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `build.rs` registers non-core category builtins under `<cat>/<name>` keys; core categories stay flat, and duplicate stems across cores panic at build time\n- New `engine/builtins.rs` helpers: `builtin_step_description`, flow-description extraction, and name lookup so `--list` and the catalog HTTP route can render namespaced entries uniformly\n- `lf/discovery.rs` rewritten around the new resolution order; the old per-source hierarchy and `skill_sources` plumbing are removed\n- `ops/gstack.rs` deleted; gstack steps, flows, and `workstyle.yaml` now live in `engine/builtins/gstack/`\n- `engine/config.rs` drops `skill_sources` (and its merge logic); docs updated in `docs/config.md` and `docs/lf.md` to describe the two remaining external channels (`npx/`, `rams/rams`)\n- `.lf/steps/refresh-gstack.md` added: pulls upstream, runs the converter into `builtins/gstack/step/`, and leaves the diff uncommitted for human review\n- `tests/discovery_tests.rs` extended, including a test that every bundled step is categorized and discoverable\n- README + `lf.md` rewritten around the new resolution order and `/` separator",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 690,
      "deletions": 1180,
      "changed_files": 65
    },
    {
      "number": 663,
      "title": "git sync: keep checked-out default branches in sync across worktrees",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo test --test git_tests sync_main_from_feature\ncargo fmt --check\ncargo clippy -- -D warnings\ncargo test --all\n```\n\nThe targeted git tests cover the two regressions: syncing from a feature worktree no longer leaves the default-branch worktree phantom-dirty, and dirty local edits on that checked-out default branch survive the sync.\n\n## Intent\n\nMake Loopflow's default-branch sync safe when the branch is checked out in a sibling worktree. `lf op pr`, `lf op sync`, `lf op next`, and other flows should be able to refresh upstream state without leaving the main worktree out of sync with its ref or discarding local edits.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The repo's canonical upstream is `origin/<default-branch>`.\n- If `stash pop` conflicts after the reset, preserving the stash for manual recovery is better than failing the sync before repairing the stale worktree state.\n- Documentation for `lf op sync` and worktree troubleshooting is the right place to explain this behavior to users.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Reset the worktree that actually has the default branch checked out instead of moving the ref with `git update-ref` alone.\n- Keep the `update-ref` fast path when the default branch is not checked out anywhere.\n- Include untracked files in the temporary stash so ad hoc local work survives the reset.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Automatic conflict resolution when restoring stashed changes.\n- Any new CLI surface area.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 175,
      "deletions": 25,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 664,
      "title": "wave reorg: collapse 13 waves into root/desktop/mobile/workflows",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nls wave/                          # root, desktop, mobile, workflows, old, roadmap\ncat wave/root/root.yaml           # area: [wave/desktop, wave/mobile, wave/workflows]\nuv run python scripts/pm_reset.py --provider asana --dry-run\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReshapes the wave tree from 13 active waves down to 4 (`root`, `desktop`, `mobile`, `workflows`) plus the `old/` archive and the `roadmap/` live-PR worktree. The old `chatgui`, `chattui`, `macos`, `ios`, `lfd`, `model`, `pm`, `gstack`, `flows`, `runboard`, and `redesign` directories are gone; their items are folded into the new homes per the plan in `scratch/wave-reorg.md`. Items collapse from 44 to 13 demo-anchored tasks, with READMEs and `*.yaml` rewritten so each wave has a single clear purpose. README and `docs/lfd.md` examples are updated from `redesign` to `conductor` to match.\n\nAlso adds `scripts/pm_reset.py`, a one-shot tool for wiping and rebuilding PM state after a reorg of this size: it deletes every loopflow-managed Asana project, clears the IDs from `wave/*/<name>.yaml`, then runs `lf op pm init --all` and `lf op pm push-diff --all`. Covered by `python/tests/test_pm_reset.py`.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `wave/desktop/`, `wave/mobile/`, `wave/workflows/` with fresh READMEs, yamls, and demo-anchored items\n- New `wave/root/1-review-open-work-and-garden-parity.md`; `root.yaml` area now points at the three new chord members\n- Deleted `wave/chatgui/`, `wave/chattui/`, `wave/macos/`, `wave/ios/`, `wave/lfd/`, `wave/model/`, `wave/pm/`, `wave/gstack/`, `wave/flows/`, `wave/runboard/`, `wave/redesign/`, and the orphan `wave/roadmap.md`\n- New `scripts/pm_reset.py` with Asana support and `--dry-run` / `--yes` flags; tests in `python/tests/test_pm_reset.py`\n- Reorg plan and assumptions captured in `scratch/wave-reorg.md` and `scratch/questions.md`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 752,
      "deletions": 4400,
      "changed_files": 116
    },
    {
      "number": 665,
      "title": "voice: checkpoint-and-proceed + design-stage framing",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nThese changes ship via the built-in prompts compiled into `loopflow`. To pick them up locally:\n\n```bash\ncargo build -p loopflow\ncargo test -p loopflow golden_prompt\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThree layered tweaks to loopflow's global prompts so agents act with less ceremony and produce design docs at the right resolution for their stage.\n\n- **Checkpoint and proceed.** Stop asking \"do you want me to get started?\" for reversible work. Snapshot with `git` if the tree is dirty, otherwise just go — commit history is the rollback. Pushes, PRs, external side effects, and destructive ops still require a check-in.\n- **Design at different stages.** Wave roadmaps and future-work sketches stay illustrative (a function name, a flow, a data shape) so they don't rot. Kickoff and review-design outputs are comprehensive — they're the spec a human reviews and an implementing agent works from.\n- **Voice.** New paragraph and banned-phrase entry in `VOICE.md` enforce the proactivity bias. `kickoff.md` and `review-design.md` get explicit \"comprehensive deliverable\" framing.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `LOOPFLOW.md`: new \"Checkpoint and proceed\" section after Commits; new \"Design at different stages\" section between Ambition and Adaptation\n- `VOICE.md`: proactivity guidance + \"Do you want me to get started on...\" added to the Never-say list\n- `build/step/kickoff.md`: \"Comprehensive over light\" principle bullet\n- `build/step/review-design.md`: end-state must be implementable without further input\n- Goldens refreshed across `tests/goldens/`\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 298,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 12
    },
    {
      "number": 666,
      "title": "op pr: add -m/--model agent override",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf -m codex op pr\nlf op pr -m codex\nlf op next\nlf op commit\n```\n\nAdds a one-off agent override for `lf op` commands that generate text via an LLM. Both the top-level `lf -m <agent>` and the per-subcommand `lf op pr -m <agent>` flags now flow through to PR copy generation, commit message generation, and the commit/PR steps inside `next` and `land`. When omitted, behavior is unchanged: `agent:` from `.lf/config.yaml` (or `~/.lf/config.yaml`) wins, falling back to `claude:opus` for PR copy and `claude:haiku` for commit messages.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `OpsCommand::Pr` gains a `-m/--model` flag (with `-M` alias); top-level `cli.model` is plumbed into `commands::ops::run`\n- `PrOptions`, `CommitOptions`, `LandOptions`, `NextOptions` each grow an `agent: Option<String>` field\n- `generate_pr_copy` and `generate_commit_message` accept an `agent_override` that takes precedence over config\n- `lf op pr` flow updated so the override survives the rebase-and-retry path\n- Tests cover both flag positions (`lf op pr -m codex` and `lf -m codex op pr`); existing call sites updated to pass `agent: None`\n- `docs/lfop.md` documents the new flag and override precedence",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 129,
      "deletions": 22,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 667,
      "title": "build(deps): bump cron from 0.12.1 to 0.16.0",
      "body": "Bumps [cron](https://github.com/zslayton/cron) from 0.12.1 to 0.16.0.\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/zslayton/cron/releases\">cron's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>v0.15.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>feat(parsing): give serviceable parsing error details to users by <a href=\"https://github.com/AlexTMjugador\"><code>@​AlexTMjugador</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/135\">zslayton/cron#135</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New Contributors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/AlexTMjugador\"><code>@​AlexTMjugador</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/135\">zslayton/cron#135</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/zslayton/cron/compare/v0.14.0...v0.15.0\">https://github.com/zslayton/cron/compare/v0.14.0...v0.15.0</a></p>\n<h2>v0.14.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>refactor(parser): Port to winnow by <a href=\"https://github.com/epage\"><code>@​epage</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/134\">zslayton/cron#134</a></li>\n<li>Fix backwards iteration when initial time has nanoseconds by <a href=\"https://github.com/canac\"><code>@​canac</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/132\">zslayton/cron#132</a></li>\n<li>chore: Add <code>cargo fmt</code> and <code>clippy</code> checks to GitHub action  by <a href=\"https://github.com/bombsimon\"><code>@​bombsimon</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/133\">zslayton/cron#133</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New Contributors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/epage\"><code>@​epage</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/134\">zslayton/cron#134</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/canac\"><code>@​canac</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/132\">zslayton/cron#132</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/zslayton/cron/compare/v0.13.0...v0.14.0\">https://github.com/zslayton/cron/compare/v0.13.0...v0.14.0</a></p>\n<h2>v0.13.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>feat: implement efficient conversion from String by <a href=\"https://github.com/LeoniePhiline\"><code>@​LeoniePhiline</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/128\">zslayton/cron#128</a></li>\n<li>Run <code>cargo fmt</code> and fix all <code>clippy</code> reports by <a href=\"https://github.com/bombsimon\"><code>@​bombsimon</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/123\">zslayton/cron#123</a></li>\n<li>Fix backwards iteration when inital time has milliseconds by <a href=\"https://github.com/juancampa\"><code>@​juancampa</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/112\">zslayton/cron#112</a></li>\n<li>Patterns with future years should not limit the day and month range by <a href=\"https://github.com/AhmedSoliman\"><code>@​AhmedSoliman</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/116\">zslayton/cron#116</a></li>\n<li>feat: implement optimized <code>Serialize</code> and <code>Deserialize</code> for <code>Schedule</code> by <a href=\"https://github.com/LeoniePhiline\"><code>@​LeoniePhiline</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/129\">zslayton/cron#129</a></li>\n<li>fix: Don't accept invalid step for <code>Period</code> by <a href=\"https://github.com/bombsimon\"><code>@​bombsimon</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/122\">zslayton/cron#122</a></li>\n<li>Iterate over rather than skipping ambigous times by <a href=\"https://github.com/AldaronLau\"><code>@​AldaronLau</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/115\">zslayton/cron#115</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New Contributors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/LeoniePhiline\"><code>@​LeoniePhiline</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/128\">zslayton/cron#128</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/bombsimon\"><code>@​bombsimon</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/123\">zslayton/cron#123</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/juancampa\"><code>@​juancampa</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/112\">zslayton/cron#112</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/AhmedSoliman\"><code>@​AhmedSoliman</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/zslayton/cron/pull/116\">zslayton/cron#116</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/zslayton/cron/compare/v0.12.1...v0.13.0\">https://github.com/zslayton/cron/compare/v0.12.1...v0.13.0</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li>See full diff in <a href=\"https://github.com/zslayton/cron/commits\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\n\n[![Dependabot compatibility score](https://dependabot-badges.githubapp.com/badges/compatibility_score?dependency-name=cron&package-manager=cargo&previous-version=0.12.1&new-version=0.16.0)](https://docs.github.com/en/github/managing-security-vulnerabilities/about-dependabot-security-updates#about-compatibility-scores)\n\nDependabot will resolve any conflicts with this PR as long as you don't alter it yourself. You can also trigger a rebase manually by commenting `@dependabot rebase`.\n\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-start)\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-end)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Dependabot commands and options</summary>\n<br />\n\nYou can trigger Dependabot actions by commenting on this PR:\n- `@dependabot rebase` will rebase this PR\n- `@dependabot recreate` will recreate this PR, overwriting any edits that have been made to it\n- `@dependabot show <dependency name> ignore conditions` will show all of the ignore conditions of the specified dependency\n- `@dependabot ignore this major version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this major version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this minor version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this minor version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this dependency` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this dependency (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n\n\n</details>",
